var text = '''[center][font_size=28][b]Color as Plurality[/b][/font_size][/center]
[center][i]The Spectrum Is Not Enough[/i][/center]

[b]The Journey So Far[/b]

We began with [b]RGB[/b] - the digital lie that three numbers equal reality.
We moved to [b]Wavelength[/b] - the physical measurement that failed to explain experience.
We explored [b]Perception[/b] - showing that different eyes see different worlds.
We encountered [b]Magenta[/b] - the impossible color that proves experience exceeds physics.

[hr]

[b]The Final Axiom[/b]

Color is not a property of an object.
Color is not a wavelength of light.
Color is not a neural signal.

**Color is an event.**

It is an event that occurs in the relationship between:
1. The physics of light
2. The biology of the eye
3. The processing of the brain
4. The categories of language
5. The context of the environment

Remove any one of these, and "color" changes or vanishes.

[hr]

[b]Queer Ontology[/b]

If reality allows for:
- Multiple valid perceptions of the same object (The Dress)
- Experiences that have no physical basis (Magenta)
- Categories that shift based on who is speaking (Blue/Green vs Ao)

Then reality is not fixed, singular, or "objective" in the way we were taught.
It is **fluid, relational, and plural**.

The algorithm tries to lock this down (Color(r, g, b)).
But the experience always escapes.

[color=pink]We build systems to measure the world, but the world is always stranger than our rulers.[/color]
